.TH AUTOLOGIN.CONF 5 2026-11-04 autologin "File Formats Manual"
.SH NAME
autologin.conf \- autologin configuration file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/autologin.conf
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B autologin.conf
tells
.BR autologin (8)
which user to log in and what to run.
.PP
The file has two parts.
The first part holds
.IB key = value
lines.
It ends with a line containing
.BR script= .
Everything after that line is the script.
.PP
In the first part, blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored, and a
.B #
after a value starts a comment.
There must be no space between the key and
.BR = .
Any other line is an error, so a misspelled key is reported instead of being run as part of the script.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.BI user= name
The user to log in.
Required.
.TP
.BR relogin= yes | no
What happens when the script exits.
With
.B yes
(the default) the user is logged in again.
With
.B no
the user is logged in only once per boot, and after that tty1 shows a normal login prompt.
.TP
.B script=
Marks where the script starts.
Required.
Anything after
.B script=
on the same line is ignored.
.SH SCRIPT
The script is passed to
.B /bin/sh \-c
as it is, including comments and lines that look like keys.
It runs as a login shell in the user's home directory, after
.B /etc/profile
and
.B ~/.profile
are read.
.PP
The script usually ends with
.BR exec ,
so the program it starts replaces the shell.
When that program exits, the session ends.
.PP
If the script is empty, an interactive shell is started on tty1.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start the mango Wayland compositor with a D-Bus session, once per boot:
.PP
.RS
.EX
# User to log in as
user=sioworker
relogin=no
script=
export XDG_RUNTIME_DIR="/run/user/$(id \-u)"
exec dbus\-run\-session mango
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check the file after editing it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
autologin \-\-check
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR autologin (8)
